  "final_text": "I finished all four steps and read the dashboard and rule back to confirm them.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Flights\" (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`) points at the `kibana_sample_data_flights` index, and its time field is `timestamp`. Its fields are:\n   - **Text (keyword):** `Carrier`, `Dest`, `DestAirportID`, `DestCityName`, `DestCountry`, `DestRegion`, `DestWeather`, `FlightDelayType`, `FlightNum`, `FlightTimeHour`, `Origin`, `OriginAirportID`, `OriginCityName`, `OriginCountry`, `OriginRegion`, `OriginWeather`\n   - **Numbers:** `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `DistanceMiles`, `FlightDelayMin`, `FlightTimeMin`, `dayOfWeek`, `hour_of_day`\n   - **True/false:** `Cancelled`, `FlightDelay`\n   - **Locations:** `DestLocation`, `OriginLocation`\n   - **Date:** `timestamp`\n   - It also has Elasticsearch's built-in fields (`_id`, `_index`, `_score`, `_source`, `_ignored`).\n\n2. **Dashboard:** I created \"S4-452c10e5 Flights\" (id `f1281887-3c04-4a5d-884e-70e8f72dff60`) with exactly two panels, both using that data view:\n   - a line chart of the number of flights over time;\n   - a bar chart of the number of flights for the top 10 carriers.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** I created \"S4-452c10e5 Cancelled flights\" (id `ecc3d001-f8d6-4690-99e5-7a8c5851d8a0`) on the flights index. It runs every minute, counts flights where `Cancelled` is true, and fires when there are more than 10 in the last 5 minutes. It has no actions, so it won't send notifications. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it.\n\n4. **Edit and read-back:** The carrier panel's title is now \"Flights by carrier\", and the dashboard still has only its two panels. Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, with the schedule and conditions above.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
